  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nWe will see an increase in moisture Sunday especially with mid \nand high clouds from the nor'easter. This will affect western PA\nand northern WV mainly. Areas closest to the Appalachian ridge-\nline could see a chance for light rain from the nor'easter. \nEastern Ohio will remain far enough west to remain mostly sunny \nsky. With the increased cloud cover, daytimes highs pull briefly\ndrop back into the upper 60s on Sunday for overcast locations. \n\nA mid level high pressure system will continue to dominate our \nweather with temperatures gradually warming daily especially \nafter Sunday. Precipitation is possible on Wednesday into\nThursday when the ridge breaks down allowing a shortwave trough\npassage. As of now, the probability of precipitation remains\nbetween 30%-40%. Based on the weak forcing with this feature \nand a relatively dry atmosphere, the probability of any precip \nshould be less than a tenth of an inch over 24 hours. Stats for \n24 hour QPF exceeding 0.10\" reside in the 20%-30% range.\n\nA surface high reaffirms itself over the Ohio Valley on Thursday\nthrough next weekend. Daytime highs outside of the mountains \ncould climb into the upper 700s by Day 7.",
